
<% content_for :title, "Information for Families — Hack Club NDA" %>
<% content_for :meta_description, "A clear, friendly guide to Hack Club's NDA for parents, guardians, and family members." %>

<div class="shell info-page">
  <header class="info-hero">
    <div class="eyebrow">HACK CLUB NDA · INFORMATION FOR FAMILIES</div>
    <h1>A little more information. A lot more clarity.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      If your teenager has been asked to sign a Non-Disclosure Agreement
      (NDA) for Hack Club, it's completely reasonable to want to understand
      what it means. This guide explains the purpose of the agreement,
      why it may be needed, and what to consider before signing.
    </p>
  </header>

  <aside class="info-note">
    <span class="info-note-label">First things first</span>
    <p>
      You do not need to understand every legal term straight away.
      Read this guide together, review the actual agreement, and ask
      questions about anything that is unclear. This page is an
      explanation, not a replacement for the agreement itself.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <section class="info-section">
    <h2>What is an NDA?</h2>
    <p>
      A Non-Disclosure Agreement is an agreement about how certain
      confidential information should be handled. It sets expectations
      for people who may encounter private information while taking
      part in an organisation's activities.
    </p>
    <p>
      Think of it as an agreement to treat information responsibly:
      to respect other people's privacy, keep protected information
      secure, and only use it for purposes that have been authorised.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="info-section">
    <h2>Why does Hack Club use one?</h2>
    <p>
      Hack Club is a community built around young people learning to
      code, making things, and organising projects and events. Some
      members and volunteers take on additional responsibilities that
      can give them access to information that should not be public.
    </p>
    <p>Depending on their role, this might include:</p>

    <div class="info-grid">
      <article class="info-card">
        <span class="info-card-number">01</span>
        <h3>Personal information</h3>
        <p>
          Contact details or other private information about members
          of the community.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="info-card">
        <span class="info-card-number">02</span>
        <h3>Internal operations</h3>
        <p>
          Information about how programmes, events, tools, and
          organisational processes are run.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="info-card">
        <span class="info-card-number">03</span>
        <h3>Private communications</h3>
        <p>
          Non-public discussions, administrative information, or
          details shared with the expectation of confidentiality.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="info-card">
        <span class="info-card-number">04</span>
        <h3>Partner information</h3>
        <p>
          Certain non-public details relating to suppliers, partners,
          or the organisation's work.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="info-small">
      These are examples, not a complete legal definition. The actual
      agreement determines what counts as confidential information.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="info-section">
    <h2>What does this mean in practice?</h2>
    <p>
      The aim is not to make participation stressful. It is to make
      sure everyone understands their responsibilities when handling
      information that belongs to other people or the organisation.
    </p>

    <div class="info-example">
      <h3>For example, someone might need to...</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Keep another member's private contact details private.</li>
        <li>Use internal tools only for their authorised purpose.</li>
        <li>Avoid sharing confidential documents or conversations publicly.</li>
        <li>Ask an appropriate person if they are unsure whether information can be shared.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p>
      The agreement should be read carefully to understand its precise
      requirements, permitted uses, exceptions, and any obligations
      that continue after participation ends.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="info-section">
    <h2>What signing does, and does not, mean</h2>

    <div class="info-grid">
      <article class="info-card">
        <h3>What it does mean</h3>
        <p>
          The signer agrees to follow the confidentiality obligations
          described in the agreement and handle covered information
          appropriately.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="info-card">
        <h3>What it does not automatically mean</h3>
        <p>
          The NDA does not, by itself, create an employment or
          independent contractor relationship with Hack Club.
          The surrounding circumstances and other agreements still matter.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p>
      An NDA is still a legal agreement. Its exact effect depends on
      its wording and the applicable law, so do not rely on this
      summary as a substitute for reading the contract.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="info-section">
    <h2>Parents and guardians: what should you know?</h2>
    <p>
      If the person signing is under 18, Hack Club's current signing
      process requires a parent or legal guardian to co-sign. The
      signer provides the co-signer's name and email address, and an
      invitation is sent to that person.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before co-signing, take the time to read the agreement itself.
      Make sure you understand the confidentiality requirements, how
      long they apply, and what the agreement says about exceptions
      and permitted disclosures.
    </p>
    <p>
      If anything is unclear, ask Hack Club for clarification before
      signing. For advice about legal rights or obligations, consider
      speaking to a qualified legal professional.
    </p>
  </section>

    <section class="info-section">
    <h2>Privacy and the signing process</h2>
    <p>
      The signing process collects personal information such as your
      legal name, email address, date of birth and home address. If a
      co-signer is required, their name and email address are collected
      too. The process also involves a video pledge and a transcript
      generated to check that the pledge matches the required wording.
    </p>
    <p>
      The application's signing form says that audio is processed by
      SpaceXAI under its Zero Data Retention (ZDR) policy. Pledge videos
      are stored in Cloudflare R2 using SSE-C encryption.
    </p>
    <p>
      When Airtable synchronisation is enabled, the application also
      sends signer details and the video transcript to Airtable. It may
      upload the pledge video and a PDF of the signed agreement there
      too, subject to the application's attachment rules.
    </p>
    <p>
      Encryption and zero-retention processing are technical safeguards,
      not a complete explanation of privacy. Before signing, make sure
      you understand how the information is used, who can access it,
      which services receive it, and how long it is kept. Ask Hack Club
      for its current privacy and retention details if anything is unclear.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="info-section info-next-steps">
    <h2>Ready to review the agreement?</h2>
    <p>
      The most useful next step is to read the actual NDA together.
      Pay particular attention to the definition of confidential
      information, the responsibilities of the signer, any exceptions,
      the duration of the obligations, and the applicable law.
    </p>

    <% if current_user %>
      <%= link_to "Go to the NDA signing page", nda_signature_path,
        class: "button button-primary" %>
    <% else %>
      <%= link_to "Sign in to review the agreement", login_path,
        class: "button button-primary" %>
    <% end %>

    <p class="info-small">
      This guide is general information only. The signed agreement
      remains the authoritative document.
    </p>
  </section>
</div>
